1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,512
Ever wondered how pressing a single key turns into a letter on your screen in milliseconds?

2
00:00:05,462 --> 00:00:13,087
Underneath every keycap sits a switch—either a rubber dome or a mechanical spring—waiting to close an electrical circuit.

3
00:00:13,087 --> 00:00:17,537
But wiring a hundred keys individually would create a tangled mess.

4
00:00:17,537 --> 00:00:23,125
Instead, keyboards use a grid of crossing rows and columns called a key matrix.

5
00:00:23,125 --> 00:00:28,325
When you press a key, it connects one specific row to one specific column.

6
00:00:28,325 --> 00:00:36,675
A tiny microcontroller constantly scans this grid, detects the exact coordinate, and translates it into a standard scan code.

7
00:00:36,675 --> 00:00:44,000
That code travels over USB or Bluetooth as a data packet, telling your operating system which character to draw.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,050
Which type of keyboard do you prefer—clicky mechanical or quiet membrane?

9
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:50,837
